<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#sosloviye">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sosloviye</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">18th century–<a href="/years/1917">1917</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as soslovie</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sosloviye was the term for the legal estates of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, introduced in the 18th and 19th centuries to approximate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s estates of the realm. The Code of Laws of 1832 defined four: the nobility and the clergy, who paid no personal tax, and the town-dwellers and the peasants, who did; the peasants, the great majority, supplied the army's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">conscripts</a>. The inorodtsy, non-Russian peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, were treated apart. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a> was abolished the estates no longer matched society, but the terms lasted until the revolution of 1917.</p></div>
